Now let's implement the frontend voice question reading logic.
In `frontend/src/app/candidate/[id]/page.tsx`, we want to speak the questions!
- When the interview starts, or when the `currentQuestionIndex` changes:
  - We synthesize the speech by:
    1. Making a request to `http://localhost:8080/api/speak?text=<text_to_speak>`.
    2. Playing the returned audio MP3 stream in the browser using the browser's dynamic `Audio` object.
    3. If that request fails, we fall back to the browser's native speech synthesis (`window.speechSynthesis`), so it works 100% of the time, even when offline!
Let's see what the speaking function looks like:
```typescript
	const speakText = async (text: string) => {
		try {
			// Try to play audio from AWS Polly backend
			const audioUrl = `${apiBase}/api/speak?text=${encodeURIComponent(text)}`;
			const audio = new Audio(audioUrl);
			audio.play().catch((playErr) => {
				console.warn('Audio autoplay failed, falling back to browser SpeechSynthesis:', playErr);
				speakTextFallback(text);
			});
		} catch (err) {
			console.warn('AWS Polly speak failed, falling back to browser SpeechSynthesis:', err);
			speakTextFallback(text);
		}
	};

	const speakTextFallback = (text: string) => {
		if (typeof window !== 'undefined' && window.speechSynthesis) {
			// Cancel any active speaking
			window.speechSynthesis.cancel();
			const utterance = new SpeechSynthesisUtterance(text);
			// Match to a natural-sounding English voice if available
			const voices = window.speechSynthesis.getVoices();
			const englishVoice = voices.find(v => v.lang.startsWith('en')) || voices[0];
			if (englishVoice) {
				utterance.voice = englishVoice;
			}
			window.speechSynthesis.speak(utterance);
		}
	};
```

